For \\(k=2\\) we have \\(T_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points is either horizontal, vertical or of slope\n\\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at least\nthree sunny lines are necessary.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nFor \\(k\\ge 3\\) we construct the required lines by induction.\n\n*Base case \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(3,2)),\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_{3}&: y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3))\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(\\frac12,-\\frac12,2\\) (all sunny) and together contain the six\npoints of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(k\\ge 3\\) the triangle \\(T_{k}\\) is covered by\n\\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  \nConsider \\(T_{k+1}\\).  The new points are those with \\(a+b=k+2\\); they form the\nouter \u201cborder\u2019\u2019  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(1,k+1),(2,k),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{k+1}:\\; y = x + (k-1) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\) (sunny) and it contains the two extreme points\n\\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((k+1,1)\\) of the border \\(B\\).  The remaining\n\\(k-1\\) points of \\(B\\) are \\((i,k+2-i)\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\); each such point\nlies on the line \\(L_{i-1}\\) of the inductive hypothesis, because that line\nalready passes through \\((i-1,1)\\) and \\((i,2)\\) and therefore contains every\npoint whose coordinates differ by the same amount.  Consequently the \\(k+1\\)\nlines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k},L_{k+1}\\) cover all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because their slopes are different (the new line has\nslope \\(1\\) while the previous \\(k\\) lines have slopes different from \\(1\\) by\nthe induction hypothesis).  This completes the induction and proves the lemma. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Choose \\(v=h=0\\) and \\(d=n-k\\) diagonal lines\n  \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1\\).  \n  By the discussion of \u00a71 the uncovered points are exactly the triangle\n  \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n* By Lemma\u202f2.1, \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff**\n  \\(k\\neq 2\\).\n\nThus a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones exists\nprecisely for the values  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Explicit constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one\n  sunny line, for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\)\n  (slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, so we use the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((n-2,2)\\), slope \\(\\frac12\\)).  All points are covered and exactly one line\n  is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k\\ge 3\\).**  Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1\\)\n  (non\u2011sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f2.1 for the triangle\n  \\(T_{k}\\).  The two families are disjoint, hence we obtain \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  No configuration exists for \\(k=2\\), and for all\nother non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) a configuration exists."
