---------------\n### 2.  Covering a triangle of size \\(k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe first settle the easy cases.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  No point has to be covered.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  The single point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line,\n  e.g. \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  The set \\(T_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Any line through two of these points is either vertical, horizontal or has\n  slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of them.\n  Hence at least three sunny lines are needed, so \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThe remaining case is the following.\n\n> **Lemma 2.1**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\) the triangle \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly\n> \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof (sketch).*\nWe construct the required lines by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(3,2)),\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\nL_{3}&: y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3))\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(\\frac12,-\\frac12,2\\) (all sunny) and together contain the six\npoints of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some \\(k\\ge 3\\) the set \\(T_{k}\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny\nlines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) whose slopes are pairwise distinct and none equal\nto \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Consider \\(T_{k+1}\\).  The new points are those with\n\\(a+b=k+2\\); they form the “border’’  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(1,k+1),(2,k),\\dots ,(k+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick an integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k\\) that is **not** the slope of any\n\\(L_{i}\\) (possible because at most \\(k\\) slopes have been used).  The line\n\n\\[\nL_{k+1}:\\; y = t\\,x + (k+2-t)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(t\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the two border points\n\\((1,k+1)\\) and \\((k+1,1)\\).  Moreover, for each interior border point\n\\((i,k+2-i)\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(k+2-i) = t\\,i + (k+2-t) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nt = \\frac{(k+2-i)-(k+2-t)}{i-1}= \\frac{t-i}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich holds precisely when \\(i=t\\).  Thus the line \\(L_{k+1}\\) contains the two\nextreme border points and exactly one additional border point, namely\n\\((t,k+2-t)\\).  The remaining \\(k-1\\) border points are already covered by the\nold lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) (each of those lines contains at least one\nborder point because the induction hypothesis covered the whole triangle\n\\(T_{k}\\)).  Consequently the \\(k+1\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k},L_{k+1}\\)\ncover all points of \\(T_{k+1}\\).\n\nAll slopes remain distinct: the new slope \\(t\\) was chosen to be different from\nthe previous ones, and none of the previous slopes equals \\(-1,0,\\infty\\).  This\ncompletes the induction and proves the lemma. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof (conditional on Lemma 2.1)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* Choose \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines as in §1 (for instance \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines\n  \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+1\\)).  By (3) the uncovered points are exactly the\n  triangle \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we have already exhibited suitable sunny lines.\n  If \\(k=2\\) Lemma 2.1 tells us that \\(T_{2}\\) cannot be covered by two sunny\n  lines, so no configuration exists.\n  If \\(k\\ge 3\\) Lemma 2.1 provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n  \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nThus, assuming Lemma 2.1, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem,\nand for \\(k=2\\) such a family does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion (conditional).**  \nIf Lemma 2.1 holds, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) has been proved\nrigorously; the existence for all other \\(k\\) reduces to the constructive\nstatement of Lemma 2.1.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
