nct sunny lines\ncovering \\(T_{t}\\).  \nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nPut \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use) and consider the\nvertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(m\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and together they contain every\npoint of \\(T_{n}\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\nThe set of points that remain uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}\\setminus\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a\\le m\\bigr\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   =T_{n-m}.\n\\]\n\nBy the hypothesis there is a covering of \\(T_{n-m}\\) by exactly \\(n-m=k\\) sunny\nlines.  Adding these \\(k\\) sunny lines to the \\(m\\) vertical lines yields a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence, **to solve the original problem it suffices to prove the existence of an\nall‑sunny covering for every size \\(t\\ge 3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  All‑sunny covering of \\(T_{t}\\) for every \\(t\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(t\\) that there exists a set\n\\(\\mathcal S_{t}=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{t}\\}\\) of \\(t\\) distinct sunny lines\ncovering \\(T_{t}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base cases  \n\n*\\(t=3\\).*  \nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}: y=3x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{3}: y=-\\frac12x+2\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(2,3,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\nthey are sunny.  Their integer points in the region \\(a+b\\le4\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&:\\;(1,1),\\;(2,3),\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&:\\;(1,2),\\;(2,5)\\;( \\text{outside }T_{3}),\\\\\n\\ell_{3}&:\\;(2,1),\\;(4,0)\\;(\\text{outside }T_{3}),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nso the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_{3}\\) are covered.\n\n*\\(t=4\\).*  \nThe four sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&: y=2x-1,\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&: y=3x-2,\\\\\n\\ell_{3}&: y=4x-3,\\\\\n\\ell_{4}&: y=-\\frac13x+\\frac43\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(2,3,4,-\\tfrac13\\) and together contain all ten points of\n\\(T_{4}\\) (a direct verification is routine).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(t\\ge 4\\) we have an all‑sunny covering\n\\(\\mathcal S_{t}=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{t}\\}\\) of \\(T_{t}\\).\nWe construct an all‑sunny covering of \\(T_{t+1}\\).\n\nDefine two new lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\ell'_{t}\\;:\\; y=(t+1)x-(t+1),\\qquad\n\\ell'_{t+1}\\;:\\; y=-\\frac1t\\,x+\\frac{t+1}{t}\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(t+1\\) and \\(-\\frac1t\\), are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n\\(\\ell'_{t},\\ell'_{t+1}\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n*Points on the new outer diagonal.*  \nThe points of \\(T_{t+1}\\) with \\(a+b=t+2\\) are\n\\[\n(1,t+1),(2,t),\\dots ,(t+1,1).\n\\]\nFor each such point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b=(t+1)a-(t+1)\\); therefore all of them\nlie on \\(\\ell'_{t}\\).\n\n*Points that were on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{t}\\) that belonged to \\(\\ell_{t}\\) satisfies\n\\(b=-\\frac1{t-1}a+\\frac{t}{t-1}\\).  Substituting \\(a\\mapsto a+1\\) shows that the\nsame integer pair \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell'_{t+1}\\);\nconsequently all points formerly covered by \\(\\ell_{t}\\) are now covered by\n\\(\\ell'_{t+1}\\).\n\n*All other points.*  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_{t}\\) are still covered by the unchanged lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{t-1}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{t+1}:=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{t-1},\\ell'_{t},\\ell'_{t+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(t+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_{t+1}\\).\nThe induction is complete.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(t\\ge 3\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_{t}\\)\nby exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nSet \\(m=n-k\\).  Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) (non‑sunny) and\nthe all‑sunny covering of the remaining triangle \\(T_{n-m}=T_{k}\\) (which exists\nby the result of Section 2).  The total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\); exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) can be realized, and no other\nvalue is possible because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total\nnumber of lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable.}}\n\\]"
