 **all** points of the new outer diagonal lie on \\(\\ell'_t\\).\n\n   *Preservation of interior points.*  \n   Every point of \\(T_t\\) that was on \\(\\ell_t\\) satisfies (4).  Re‑writing (4) as  \n\n   \\[\n   b' = -\\frac{1}{t-1}a' + \\frac{t+1}{t-1},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   and replacing \\(a'\\) by \\(a'+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b' = -\\frac{1}{t}a' + \\frac{t+1}{t},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the equation of \\(\\ell'_{t+1}\\) in (5).  Consequently each integer pair that lay on \\(\\ell_t\\) now lies on \\(\\ell'_{t+1}\\).\n\n   *Unchanged lines.*  \n   The remaining points of \\(T_t\\) stay on the unchanged lines\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{t-1}\\).\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{t+1}:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{t-1},\\ell'_t,\\ell'_{t+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_{t+1}\\).  Moreover \\(\\ell'_{t+1}\\) has the same shape as (4) with \\(t\\) replaced by \\(t+1\\).  This completes the induction, establishing the lemma for all \\(t\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_2\\) consists of the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two of these points is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are *non‑sunny*.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points in (8).  To cover all three points we would need at least three sunny lines.  Consequently a configuration with exactly two sunny lines can never cover the whole triangle \\(T_2\\), and therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * For \\(k=0\\): use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   * For \\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and a single sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   * For \\(k\\ge 3\\):  \n     – take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) (non‑sunny);  \n     – by the Lemma, cover the triangle \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines;  \n     – translate those \\(k\\) lines by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) so that they lie in the original coordinate system and together with the vertical lines cover all points required.  \n   * The case \\(k=2\\) is excluded by the impossibility argument above.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The construction shows existence, and the argument for \\(k=2\\) shows non‑existence, completing the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
