nequality forces \\(q+p\\ge 1\\).  \n   Because \\(q\\ge 1\\) and \\(|p|\\ge 1\\), the only way to have **two** distinct interior points on the same line is to be able to take both \\(t=-1\\) and \\(t=-2\\).  \n   The condition for \\(t=-2\\) would be  \n\n   \\[\n   i-2q\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-2p\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n\n   which together imply \\(2(q+p)\\le 1\\).  \n   This is impossible when \\(q+p\\ge 1\\) and \\(q,p\\) are integers unless \\(q+p=1\\) and \\(q=p=0\\), contradicting \\(|p|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Hence **at most one interior lattice point can lie on a sunny line that already contains a hypotenuse point**, provided the triangle has side‑length \\(k\\ge 4\\).  \n   (For \\(k=3\\) the inequality \\(i-2q\\ge 1\\) can still hold for some \\(i\\); this is why the case \\(k=3\\) is exceptional.)\n\n   *Step 3 – counting interior points.*  \n   The triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, of which the hypotenuse contributes \\(k\\) points.  \n   Therefore the number of interior points (those with \\(a+b\\le k\\)) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   I_k = |T_k|-k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   From Step 2 each of the \\(k\\) lines can cover **at most one** interior point when \\(k\\ge 4\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\(k\\).  \n   For \\(k\\ge 4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   I_k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2} > k,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  \n   Hence no family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover \\(T_k\\) when \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – the case \\(k=2\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_2\\) consists of the three non‑collinear points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Any line that contains two of them is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); all three are non‑sunny.  \n   Therefore a covering with two sunny lines is impossible, confirming the general impossibility for \\(k=2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The reduction (★) together with the constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) and the impossibility proof for every other \\(k\\) yields the complete answer: the only admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1,3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle covering problem\n\nTake \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on one of them, so the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+i\\;(i\\ge 1)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(m+i)+b\\le n+1\\Longleftrightarrow i+b\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(i,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **(★)** For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_k\\)?\n\nIf the answer to (★) is “yes”, then adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones; conversely, any configuration of \\(n\\) lines with \\(k\\) sunny lines can be transformed (by discarding the non‑sunny lines) into a solution of (★).  \nTherefore solving (★) for each \\(k\\) determines all admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n  The line \\(\\ell\\colon y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Adding the remaining \\(n-1\\) vertical lines yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  None of them is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Their incidences are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ it contains}\\\\ \\hline\n  \\ell_1 & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n  \\ell_2 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n  \\ell_3 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(T_3\\) is covered.  Adding the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\nThus \\(k=0,1,3\\) are admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility for all other \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Every hypotenuse point needs its own line\n\nThe **hypotenuse** of the triangle \\(T_k\\) is  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\bigl\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any two distinct points of \\(H_k\\) the line through them has slope \\(-1\\); such a line is forbidden.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_k\\).  \nSince a covering must contain all points of \\(H_k\\), any family of sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\) must contain at least \\(|H_k|=k\\) lines.  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, **each line must contain exactly one point of \\(H_k\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. At most one interior point per line when \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nFix a hypotenuse point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,k+1-i)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k)\n\\]\n\nand let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line through \\(P_i\\).  \nWrite the slope of \\(\\ell\\) in lowest terms as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\),\n\n\\[\n|p|\\ge 1,\\qquad q\\ge 1,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on \\(\\ell\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\nP_i + t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIncreasing \\(t\\) by \\(1\\) changes the sum of the coordinates by \\(q+p\\).  \nTo stay inside the triangle we must move **towards** smaller sums, therefore we need  \n\n\\[\nq+p>0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe first interior lattice point (if any) corresponds to \\(t=-1\\) in (1) and is  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(i-q,\\;k+1-i-p).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(Q_i\\) to belong to \\(T_k\\) we need  \n\n\\[\ni-q\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-p\\ge 1,\\qquad (i-q)+(k+1-i-p)=k+1-(q+p)\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThe last inequality is exactly condition (2).  \nThus **at most one** interior point can be obtained by taking \\(t=-1\\).\n\nSuppose a second interior point existed, i.e. \\(t=-2\\) also satisfies the three inequalities.  \nThen we would have  \n\n\\[\ni-2q\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-2p\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich together imply  \n\n\\[\n2(q+p)\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q\\) and \\(|p|\\) are positive integers, the left‑hand side is at least \\(2\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **no sunny line that already contains a hypotenuse point can contain two distinct interior points** when the triangle has side‑length \\(k\\ge 4\\) (the argument uses only the integrality of \\(p,q\\) and does not depend on the actual value of \\(k\\) beyond the fact that the inequalities above are meaningful).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3. Counting interior points\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.  \nSubtracting the \\(k\\) hypotenuse points leaves  \n\n\\[\nI_k = |T_k|-k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\ninterior points (those with \\(a+b\\le k\\)).\n\nFrom §3.2 each of the \\(k\\) lines can cover **at most one** interior point when \\(k\\ge 4\\).  \nTherefore the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\(k\\).  \nBut for every \\(k\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\nI_k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2} > k .\n\\]\n\nThus it is impossible to cover all points of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines when \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4. The case \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line containing two of these points is either \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) or \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); all three are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently a covering with two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Synthesis\n\nThe analysis yields:\n\n* \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realizable (explicit constructions given in §2);\n* \\(k=2\\) is impossible (§3.4);\n* every \\(k\\ge 4\\) is impossible (§3.3).\n\nTherefore, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand only these values can occur as the number of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition."
