e remaining \\(n-1\\) vertical lines yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  None of them is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Their incidences are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ it contains}\\\\ \\hline\n  \\ell_1 & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n  \\ell_2 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n  \\ell_3 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(T_3\\) is covered.  Adding the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\nThus \\(k=0,1,3\\) are admissible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility for all other \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Every hypotenuse point needs its own line\n\nThe **hypotenuse** of the triangle \\(T_k\\) is  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\bigl\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any two distinct points of \\(H_k\\) the line through them has slope \\(-1\\); such a line is forbidden.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_k\\).  \nSince a covering must contain all points of \\(H_k\\), any family of sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\) must contain at least \\(|H_k|=k\\) lines.  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, **each line must contain exactly one point of \\(H_k\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. At most one interior point per line when \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nFix a hypotenuse point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,k+1-i)\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k)\n\\]\n\nand let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line through \\(P_i\\).  \nWrite the slope of \\(\\ell\\) in lowest terms as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\),\n\n\\[\n|p|\\ge 1,\\qquad q\\ge 1,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on \\(\\ell\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\nP_i + t\\,(q,p),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIncreasing \\(t\\) by \\(1\\) changes the sum of the coordinates by \\(q+p\\).  \nTo stay inside the triangle we must move **towards** smaller sums, therefore we need  \n\n\\[\nq+p>0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe first interior lattice point (if any) corresponds to \\(t=-1\\) in (1) and is  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(i-q,\\;k+1-i-p).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(Q_i\\) to belong to \\(T_k\\) we need  \n\n\\[\ni-q\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-p\\ge 1,\\qquad (i-q)+(k+1-i-p)=k+1-(q+p)\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThe last inequality is exactly condition (2).  \nThus **at most one** interior point can be obtained by taking \\(t=-1\\).\n\nSuppose a second interior point existed, i.e. \\(t=-2\\) also satisfies the three inequalities.  \nThen we would have  \n\n\\[\ni-2q\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-2p\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich together imply  \n\n\\[\n2(q+p)\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q\\) and \\(|p|\\) are positive integers, the left\u2011hand side is at least \\(2\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **no sunny line that already contains a hypotenuse point can contain two distinct interior points** when the triangle has side\u2011length \\(k\\ge 4\\) (the argument uses only the integrality of \\(p,q\\) and does not depend on the actual value of \\(k\\) beyond the fact that the inequalities above are meaningful).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3. Counting interior points\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.  \nSubtracting the \\(k\\) hypotenuse points leaves  \n\n\\[\nI_k = |T_k|-k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\ninterior points (those with \\(a+b\\le k\\)).\n\nFrom \u00a73.2 each of the \\(k\\) lines can cover **at most one** interior point when \\(k\\ge 4\\).  \nTherefore the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\(k\\).  \nBut for every \\(k\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\nI_k = \\frac{k(k-1)}{2} > k .\n\\]\n\nThus it is impossible to cover all points of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines when \\(k\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4. The case \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line containing two of these points is either \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) or \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)); all three are non\u2011sunny.  \nConsequently a covering with two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Synthesis\n\nThe analysis yields:\n\n* \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realizable (explicit constructions given in \u00a72);\n* \\(k=2\\) is impossible (\u00a73.4);\n* every \\(k\\ge 4\\) is impossible (\u00a73.3).\n\nTherefore, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand only these values can occur as the number of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition."

