            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle covering problem\n\nTake \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on one of them, so the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+i\\;(i\\ge 1)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(m+i)+b\\le n+1\\Longleftrightarrow i+b\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(i,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **(★)** For a given non‑negative integer \\(k\\) does there exist a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_k\\)?\n\nIf the answer to (★) is “yes”, then adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones; conversely, any configuration of \\(n\\) lines with \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a solution of (★) after discarding the non‑sunny lines.  \nTherefore solving (★) for each \\(k\\) determines all admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \n  The line \\(\\ell\\colon y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Adding the remaining \\(n-1\\) vertical lines yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The set  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n  \\]\n\n  is covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  None of them is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Their incidences are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ it contains}\\\\ \\hline\n  \\ell_1 & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n  \\ell_2 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n  \\ell_3 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(T_3\\) is covered.  Adding the remaining \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Structural lemmas for a covering of \\(T_k\\)\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nH_k=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points on the hypotenuse of the triangle (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (hypotenuse points)\n\nA sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(H_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((i,k+1-i)\\) and \\((j,k+1-j)\\) of \\(H_k\\) then its slope would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(k+1-j)-(k+1-i)}{j-i}= -1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently, any family of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\) must contain exactly one point of \\(H_k\\) on each line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (interior points on a line through a hypotenuse point)\n\nLet \\(P_i=(i,k+1-i)\\in H_k\\) and let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line through \\(P_i\\).  \nWrite the slope of \\(\\ell\\) in lowest terms as \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nq>0,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\qquad |p|\\ge 1,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points of \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_i+t\\,(q,p)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIncreasing \\(t\\) by \\(1\\) changes the sum of the coordinates by \\(q+p\\).  \nBecause points of \\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), moving **into** the triangle requires the sum to decrease; hence  \n\n\\[\nq+p\\ge 2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nStarting from the hypotenuse point (\\(t=0\\)), the largest integer \\(t<0\\) for which the point stays inside the triangle is obtained from the inequalities  \n\n\\[\ni-tq\\ge 1,\\qquad k+1-i-tp\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n-t\\le\\frac{i-1}{q},\\qquad -t\\le\\frac{k-i}{p}\\quad(p>0),\n\\]\n\nor, when \\(p<0\\),\n\n\\[\n-t\\le\\frac{i-1}{q},\\qquad -t\\le\\frac{k-i}{|p|}.\n\\]\n\nIn every case the admissible negative values of \\(t\\) are  \n\n\\[\nt=-1,-2,\\dots , -\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{\\,q+p\\,}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus the number of **interior** lattice points of \\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\ns_i=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{\\,q+p\\,}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is maximal when \\(q+p\\) is minimal.  \nBecause of (5) the smallest possible value of \\(q+p\\) is \\(2\\).  \nThe only reduced fraction with \\(q+p=2\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{1}\\) (slope \\(1\\)); the alternative \\(\\frac{-1}{3}\\) does not satisfy \\(q+p=2\\).  \nHence a line can contain **two** interior points of \\(T_k\\) **only** when its slope is \\(1\\).\n\nA line of slope \\(1\\) through a hypotenuse point exists exactly when the hypotenuse point is the centre of the hypotenuse, i.e. when  \n\n\\[\ni=k+1-i\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad i=\\frac{k+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich occurs precisely for odd \\(k\\).  For even \\(k\\) every sunny line through a hypotenuse point has \\(q+p\\ge 3\\) and therefore contributes at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ninterior points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Counting interior points\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nlattice points; the hypotenuse contributes \\(k\\) of them, so the number of interior points is  \n\n\\[\nI_k=|T_k|-k=\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1. Even \\(k\\ge 4\\)\n\nFor even \\(k\\) Lemma 2 gives \\(q+p\\ge 3\\) for every line, whence each line covers at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{k-2}{3}\n\\]\n\ninterior points.  \nWith \\(k\\) lines the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\frac{k-2}{3}= \\frac{k(k-2)}{3}\n   <\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}=I_k\\qquad(k\\ge 4).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(k\\ge 4\\) the interior points of \\(T_k\\) cannot all be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2. Odd \\(k\\ge 5\\)\n\nExactly one line (the one through the middle hypotenuse point) may have slope \\(1\\); by (6) it can contain  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k-1}{2}\n\\]\n\ninterior points.  \nAll remaining \\(k-1\\) lines have \\(q+p\\ge 3\\) and therefore each contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{k-2}{3}\\) interior points.  \nConsequently the total number of interior points that can be covered does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k-1}{2}+(k-1)\\frac{k-2}{3}\n   =\\frac{2k^{2}-5k+3}{6}\n   <\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}=I_k\\qquad(k\\ge 5).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence for odd \\(k\\ge 5\\) a covering of all interior points is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3. The case \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line containing two of these points is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); all three are non‑sunny.  \nTherefore a covering with two sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Completion of the proof for the original problem\n\nBy the reduction in § 1, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** the triangle‑covering problem (★) has a solution.  \nLemma 1 forces each sunny line to contain a distinct hypotenuse point; Lemma 2 together with the counting arguments (8)–(9) shows that (★) is impossible for every \\(k\\ge 4\\).  \nThe explicit constructions of § 2 prove that (★) is possible for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\nTherefore the only admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\nThis holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
