xactly the set \\(T_{\\,n-2}\\).  \nConsequently the original problem for the parameter \\(n\\) reduces to the same problem for the smaller parameter \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFrom \u00a71 we know that at least two of the \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\); for \\(n=3\\) at least one line is non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines can never exceed  \n\n\\[\nn-2\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines (1).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;\\text{the line through }(t,t)\\text{ and }(t+1,t-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_t\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{1}{t-1}\\); it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \nThe family \\(\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nWe claim that the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\}\\) together with \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a=b\\), then it lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n*If \\(a<b\\), write \\(b-a = t-1\\) with \\(t=b-a+1\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)); then \\((a,b)=(t, t+(t-1))\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  \n*If \\(a>b\\), set \\(t=b+1\\); then \\((a,b)=(t+(a-b-1),t-1)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of the interior triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) is covered, and the two edge\u2011lines cover the points on the left and bottom edges.  Hence we have a configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe three points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nChoose the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich together cover the three points.  Thus the bound \\(n-2=1\\) is attained.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining every smaller number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the configuration of \u00a74, which contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nPick any sunny line \\(L\\) of that configuration.  \nBecause \\(L\\) contains at least one point of \\(T_n\\), we may replace \\(L\\) by a non\u2011sunny line that also passes through that point (for instance a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with a suitable integer \\(c\\)).  \nThe new line still covers the point that was lost, and all other points remain covered.  \nThus the replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\nRepeating this operation we can reduce the number of sunny lines from \\(n-2\\) down to any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe extreme case \\(k=0\\) is obtained directly by using only non\u2011sunny lines, e.g.\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5,\\dots ,x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich are \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), and no larger number is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\}}\n\\] \n\nThis completes the solution. \u220e"
