\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\nsatisfies \\(x=a\\) for some \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the \u201call\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 problem\n\nAssume that for a certain integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of\n\\(T_m\\).\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nPlace the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(their number is \\(n-k\\)).  These lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  The uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b' = b\\) we see that this set is\n\\(T_k\\).  By hypothesis there is a family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines\ncovering \\(T_k\\).  Adding the lines (1) to \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) yields \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.\n\nConsequently, **the existence of an all\u2011sunny configuration for every size\n\\(m\\) implies the existence of a configuration with any prescribed number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  All\u2011sunny configurations for every size\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\ge 3\\) that there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Base cases  \n\n*\\(m=3\\).*  \nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=-\\frac12x+\\frac32 ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y= x ,\\\\\nL_3 &: y=-2x+5\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nare sunny (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,1,-2\\)).  Their point\u2011sets are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nL_2\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\nL_3\\cap T_3 &=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich together constitute the whole set \\(T_3\\).\n\n*\\(m=4\\).*  \nThe four sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=-\\frac13x+\\frac43 ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52 ,\\\\\nL_3 &: y= x ,\\\\\nL_4 &: y=-3x+7\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(-\\tfrac13,-\\tfrac12,1,-3\\) and intersect \\(T_4\\) in the\nfollowing disjoint subsets:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\{(1,3),(4,1)\\},\\\\\nL_2 &: \\{(1,4),(3,2)\\},\\\\\nL_3 &: \\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\},\\\\\nL_4 &: \\{(2,4),(1,2)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese four subsets together contain the ten points of \\(T_4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 5\\) an all\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) of\n\\(m-2\\) lines covering \\(T_{m-2}\\) is already known.  We construct a family\n\\(\\mathcal S_m\\) for \\(T_m\\).\n\nConsider the two new points \\((m,1)\\) and \\((1,m)\\) that belong to \\(T_m\\) but\nnot to \\(T_{m-2}\\).  Define\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_a &: \\text{the line through }(1,m)\\text{ and }(m-1,2),\\\\\nL_b &: \\text{the line through }(m,1)\\text{ and }(2,m-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines have slope \\(-\\dfrac{m-2}{\\,m-1\\,}\\); this number is different from\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(m\\ge 3\\), hence \\(L_a\\) and \\(L_b\\)\nare sunny.  Moreover\n\n\\[\nL_a\\cap T_m =\\{(1,m),(2,m-1),\\dots ,(m-1,2)\\},\n\\qquad\nL_b\\cap T_m =\\{(m,1),(m-1,2),\\dots ,(2,m-1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_m\\) either lies on \\(L_a\\) or on \\(L_b\\), or\nhas both coordinates at most \\(m-2\\); the latter points constitute exactly\n\\(T_{m-2}\\).  The latter set is already covered by the lines of\n\\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m = \\mathcal S_{m-2}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_a,L_b\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((m-2)+2=m\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_m\\).\nThe induction is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of exactly \\(m\\)\n  sunny lines covering the set \\(T_m\\).\n* Using this configuration together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\)\n  we obtain, for any \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a\n  configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines in which precisely \\(k\\) of them are\n  sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n  least one of the lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
