* If \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_d\\) by definition.\n\n   * If \\(d\\ge k\\) then \\(b\\ge a+k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     2a\\le n+1-k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   * If \\(d\\le -1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\).  \n     Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n     \\[\n     2a\\ge n+2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\ge\\frac{n+2}{2}\\ge k+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Consequently \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and again \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).\n\n   In all three cases the point belongs to one of the chosen lines, proving that the family of \\(n\\) lines indeed covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, so every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   is attainable.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition derived from the counting argument coincides with the sufficient condition given by the explicit construction; therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those described above.  \n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nAmong all sunny lines the one that can contain the most points of \\(S_n\\) is a line of slope \\(1\\); such a line consists of the points with a fixed difference \\(b-a\\).  \nFor a fixed difference \\(d\\) the admissible points satisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-d,\\qquad b=a+d\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nhence the number of points on the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b-a=d\\}=n+1-d-\\max\\{1,1-d\\}\n\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** sunny line contains at most \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints, while each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (a line parallel to one side of the triangle can pass through at most the whole side, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|\n      =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA short computation gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (5).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=x+i .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical lines, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\nWe claim that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{L_i\\mid 0\\le i\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and put \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_d\\) by (6).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(d\\ge k\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na+(a+d)\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow 2a\\le n+1-d\\le n+1-k,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\), the right\u2011hand side is at most \\(n\\), and in particular \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \nThus \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (7).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(d\\le -1\\).  \nNow \\(b\\le a-1\\). Using again \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na+(a-1)\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow 2a\\le n+2\\Longrightarrow a\\ge\\frac{n+2}{2}\\ge k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\) is again in \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_a\\).\n\nAll three possibilities place \\((a,b)\\) on a line of the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) indeed covers \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the \\(V_j\\)\u2019s).  \nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (5) with the explicit construction, we obtain that the required configuration exists **iff**\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence the set of all admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
