            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑isosceles\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nConsider a sunny line of slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nAmong all sunny lines the one that can contain the most points of \\(S_n\\) is a\nline of slope \\(1\\); such a line is described by  \n\n\\[\ny=x+d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the admissible points satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\nb=a+d\\ge1,\\\\[2pt]\na+b=2a+d\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\Bigl[\\max\\{1,1-d\\},\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr],\n\\]\n\nand the number of integer \\(a\\) in this interval equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\\max\\{1,1-d\\}+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line contains at most**  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) can intersect the triangle only along one of its sides; consequently it\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nNow suppose that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\)\nand that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds above we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\,s+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   \\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\n   \\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}. \\tag{U}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\) satisfies (U).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+i . \\tag{S}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n\\(L_i\\) consists precisely of the points of \\(S_n\\) whose difference\n\\(b-a\\) equals \\(i\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines\n\nFor every integer \\(t\\) with \\(k+1\\le t\\le n\\) we have at our disposal the two\nnon‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV_t:\\;x=t ,\\qquad H_t:\\;y=t .\n\\]\n\nWe shall **choose** exactly one of the two for each \\(t\\); the total number of\nchosen lines is therefore \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Proof that the chosen family covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\in L_d\\) by definition, so the point is covered.\n\n*Case 2 – \\(d\\ge k\\).*  \nHere \\(b\\ge a+k\\). From the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-d\\le n+1-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nWe **select** the vertical line \\(V_a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).\n\n*Case 3 – \\(d\\le -1\\).*  \nNow \\(a\\ge b+1\\). Using again \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n2b\\le n+1-(a-b)\\le n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nWe **select** the horizontal line \\(H_b\\); then \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).\n\nThe three cases exhaust all possibilities, so every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one\nof the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\cup\\{V_t,H_t\\}_{t=k+1}^{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  \nAmong the \\(2(n-k)\\) candidates \\(V_t,H_t\\) we keep precisely one for each\n\\(t\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore we add exactly \\(n-k\\) further lines.\nAll lines are distinct, so the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (U) we have produced a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe inequality (U) is necessary, and the construction above shows that it is\nalso sufficient.  Consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
