f “sunny”. Correct.\n\n#### Step 3 – Upper bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n> “Because we are allowed to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, clearly \\(k\\le n\\).”\n\n*Justification:* Trivial. Correct.\n\n#### Step 4 – Construction of the non‑sunny part\n> “Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\dots,\\ell_{\\,n-k}:x=n-k\\). These lines are non‑sunny and together they cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).”\n\n*Justification:* Each point with \\(a\\) in that range lies on exactly one of those vertical lines. Correct.\n\n#### Step 5 – Description of the remaining region\n> “After removing those columns, the uncovered points are exactly  \n> \\(S_{k}^{\\prime}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n> By the change of variables \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(1\\le a'\\le k\\)) we obtain a bijection \\((a,b)\\leftrightarrow (a',b)\\) with \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\). Hence \\(S_{k}^{\\prime}\\) is a copy of the set \\(S_{k}\\).”\n\n*Justification:* Simple translation of the \\(x\\)-coordinate; the inequalities transform correctly. This step is valid.\n\n#### Step 6 – Statement of Lemma 1\n> “Lemma 1. For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(S_{m}\\).”\n\n*Justification:* This is the pivotal claim. Its proof is examined next.\n\n#### Step 7 – Definition of the lines \\(L_i\\)\n> “For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) define  \n> \\(L_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (m+1-i) - i.\\)”\n\n*Simplification:* The constant term is \\(m+1-2i\\), so \\(L_i: y = i x + (m+1-2i)\\).\n\n*Verification of sunny property:*  \nSlope \\(i\\ge1\\) ⇒ not \\(0\\), not \\(-1\\), finite ⇒ not vertical. Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny. This part is correct.\n\n#### Step 8 – Claim that every point of \\(S_m\\) lies on one of the lines\nThe solution proceeds with several algebraic manipulations:\n\n1. **Choice of index:** “Let \\((a,b)\\in S_m\\); set \\(i:=b\\).”\n2. **Inequality:** “