 = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered set is precisely the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b')\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a copy of \\(T_k\\) (the same shape, only shifted).  \nThus, after fixing the vertical lines (2), the remaining task is to cover the points of \\(T_k'\\) with **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Covering a triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\nFor the triangle \\(T_k'\\) we use the following family of lines, all of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;L_j:\\; y = x + (j-1)\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_j\\) is distinct because the intercept \\(j-1\\) is different.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nEvery point \\((a',b')\\in T_k'\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_j\\) defined in (4).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a given \\((a',b')\\) set  \n\n\\[\nj = b' - a' + 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a',b')\\in T_k'\\) we have \\(a'\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\).  \nFrom \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b' - a' + 1 \\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer \\(j\\) defined by (5) satisfies \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  \nNow substitute \\(j\\) into the equation of \\(L_j\\):\n\n\\[\ny = x + (j-1)=x + (b'-a') .\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\((x,y)=(a',b')\\) gives \\(b' = a' + (b'-a')\\), which is true.  \nHence \\((a',b')\\) belongs to \\(L_j\\). ∎\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover **all** points of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Assembling the whole configuration  \n\nCombine the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (2) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (4).  \nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); they are pairwise distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope while the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either has first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) and is covered by a vertical line, or has first coordinate \\(\\ge n-k+1\\) and, after the shift described in Section 3, belongs to \\(T_k'\\) and is therefore covered by one of the lines \\(L_j\\) (Lemma 1).  \n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are not sunny. Hence the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (1) we know that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction of Sections 3–5 shows that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realized.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
