at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the lines (5).\n\nThe points that are **not** covered by (5) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{Q}_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the right‑angled triangular lattice \\(T_k\\) of size \\(k\\) (obtained from \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) by the translation\n\\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\)).  \nThus it remains to cover \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Covering the triangle \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Base case \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal{Q}_0=\\varnothing\\); the empty family of lines (zero sunny lines) covers it.\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the claim holds for \\(k-1\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \nConsider \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) and the point  \n\n\\[\nP_k=(k,1)\\in\\mathcal{Q}_k .\n\\]\n\nChoose an **irrational** number \\(\\alpha_k\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_k=\\sqrt{2}+k\\)) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_k:\\; y-1=\\alpha_k\\,(x-k)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nbe the line through \\(P_k\\) with slope \\(\\alpha_k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha_k\\) is irrational, \\(\\ell_k\\) contains no lattice point other than \\(P_k\\); in particular it meets \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) only at \\(P_k\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha_k\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(\\ell_k\\) is sunny.\n\nRemove \\(P_k\\) from \\(