g the triangle \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) the set \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Base case \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal{Q}_0=\\varnothing\\); the empty family of lines (zero sunny lines) covers it.\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the claim holds for \\(k-1\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \nConsider \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) and the point  \n\n\\[\nP_k=(k,1)\\in\\mathcal{Q}_k .\n\\]\n\nChoose an **irrational** number \\(\\alpha_k\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_k=\\sqrt{2}+k\\)) and let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_k:\\; y-1=\\alpha_k\\,(x-k)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nbe the line through \\(P_k\\) with slope \\(\\alpha_k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha_k\\) is irrational, \\(\\ell_k\\) contains no lattice point other than \\(P_k\\); in particular it meets \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) only at \\(P_k\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha_k\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the line \\(\\ell_k\\) is sunny.\n\nRemove \\(P_k\\) from \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\).  The remaining set splits into two disjoint parts:\n\n* the vertical column  \n\n  \\[\n  V_k:=\\{(k,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le k\\},\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  which is a subset of the vertical line \\(x=k\\) (a non‑sunny line);\n\n* the triangle  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal{Q}_{k-1}' :=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k-1,\\;1\\le b\\le k-a\\},\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  which is exactly \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{k-1}\\) after a translation of the coordinate system.\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{k-1}'\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \nAdding the vertical line \\(x=k\\) (which does **not** increase the number of sunny lines) and the sunny line \\(\\ell_k\\) yields a covering of the whole set \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(k\\), completing the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final assembly\n\nFor the original integer \\(n\\) and the chosen \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)):\n\n* use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) – all non‑sunny;\n* cover the remaining triangle \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the claim proved in Section 3.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) belongs either to a column \\(x\\le n-k\\) (hence lies on one of the lines (5)) or, after the translation described in Section 2, belongs to \\(\\mathcal{Q}_k\\) and is therefore on one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAll lines are distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope whereas the sunny lines have irrational slopes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\);\n* for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
