n on \\(k\\) the following claim.\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines such that every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n#### Base case \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal Q_0=\\varnothing\\); the empty family (zero sunny lines) satisfies the claim.\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the claim holds for \\(k-1\\) (\\(k\\ge1\\)).  \nDecompose \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) as\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k = \\mathcal Q_{k-1}\\;\\cup\\;H_k,\n\\qquad\nH_k:=\\{(i,k+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe two sets are disjoint because points of \\(H_k\\) satisfy \\(a+b=k+1\\) whereas points of \\(\\mathcal Q_{k-1}\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le k\\).\n\n*By the induction hypothesis* there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) of \\(k-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q_{k-1}\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a distinct irrational number \\(\\alpha_i\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=\\sqrt{2}+i\\)) and define\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-(k+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha_i\\) is irrational, the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains no lattice point other than \\((i,k+1-i)\\); consequently \\(\\ell_i\\) meets \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) only at that point.  \nSince \\(\\alpha_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k-1+k = k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct).  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{k-1}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\); every point of \\(H_k\\) is covered by the corresponding \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers the whole \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final construction for the original problem\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n\n1. Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n2. Apply the claim of Section 3 to obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  \n\nThe union of the two families contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n\n*Coverage verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n- If \\(a\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from step 1.  \n- If \\(a>n-k\\), write \\(a=(n-k)+a'\\) with \\(a'\\ge1\\).  \n  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\((a',b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\).  \n  By the property of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), \\((a',b)\\) lies on some sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n  Translating \\(\\ell\\) back by adding \\(n-k\\) to the \\(x\\)-coordinate yields a line that contains the original point \\((a,b)\\).  \n  Since the translation does not change the slope, the line remains sunny and belongs to our configuration.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\).  \n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
