scription of all possible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(A\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), \\(B\\) on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and \\(C\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); all three directions are non‑sunny.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(A\\), their difference vector would be vertical, i.e. parallel to \\((0,1)\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for \\(B\\) (horizontal) and for \\(C\\) (direction \\((1,-1)\\)). ∎\n\nConsequently, to cover all points of \\(A\\) we need at least one line that is not sunny; the same holds for \\(B\\) and for \\(C\\).  \n\nWhen \\(n\\ge 4\\) the three sets are pairwise disjoint (they intersect only at the three vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\)), hence three *different* non‑sunny lines are required. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-3\\qquad (n\\ge 4)} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the three sets intersect, so the bound (1) is not sharp; we treat this case later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nAssume \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{2}:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{3}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThey cover all points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).  \nThe *interior* points are  \n\n\\[\nS_{\\text{int}}:=S_{n}\\setminus (A\\c