er bound proved in step\u202f2 yields the complete description of all possible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(A\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), \\(B\\) on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), and \\(C\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); all three directions are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(A\\), their difference vector would be vertical, i.e. parallel to \\((0,1)\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. The same argument works for \\(B\\) (horizontal) and for \\(C\\) (direction \\((1,-1)\\)). \u220e\n\nConsequently, to cover all points of \\(A\\) we need at least one line that is not sunny; the same holds for \\(B\\) and for \\(C\\).  \n\nWhen \\(n\\ge 4\\) the three sets are pairwise disjoint (they intersect only at the three vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\)), hence three *different* non\u2011sunny lines are required. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-3\\qquad (n\\ge 4)} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the three sets intersect, so the bound (1) is not sharp; we treat this case later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nAssume \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{2}:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{3}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThey cover all points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).  \nThe *interior* points are  \n\n\\[\nS_{\\text{int}}:=S_{n}\\setminus (A\\cup B\\cup C)\n          =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_{\\text{int}}|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-|A|-|B|-|C|+|A\\cap B|+|A\\cap C|+|B\\cap C|\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-2\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}:\\; y = x + (t-2). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_{t}\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_{\\text{int}}\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\) iff \\(b-a=t-2\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\), the admissible values of \\(a\\) are  \n\n\\[\n2\\le a\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{t}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_{\\text{int}}\\).  \n\nSumming over all \\(t\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=2}^{\\,n-2}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-t}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 =|S_{\\text{int}}| ,\n\\]\n\nso every interior point belongs to precisely one of the lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  \n\nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}:=\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(3+(n-3)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are non\u2011sunny.  \nTherefore for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3\n\\]\n\nexists. For \\(n\\ge 4\\) this value is maximal by (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining all smaller numbers of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) (which has \\(k=n-3\\)), replace any sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) by a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=c\\)) that passes through a point already covered by another line of the family.  \nThe replacement does not destroy the covering property, but it reduces the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n\nRepeating this operation we can achieve any integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge 4\\) the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nS_{3}= \\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three special sets are  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\},\\;\nB=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\},\\;\nC=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause they intersect pairwise, only two non\u2011sunny lines are needed.  \nExplicit families are:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\}\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(\\{y=x\\ (\\text{sunny}),\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\);\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=x+1\\ (\\text{both sunny}),\\;x+y=4\\}\\).\n\nA third non\u2011sunny line is unavoidable to cover the three points of \\(C\\); consequently \\(k=3\\) is impossible.  \n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
