\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is always feasible.\n\n3. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line can contain at most one of these six points (a direct check of all possible slopes shows that any line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) in at most one point).  \n   With three lines we can therefore cover at most three points if at least one line is sunny, which is insufficient. Consequently all three lines must be non‑sunny and the only admissible value is \\(k=0\\).\n\n4. **Upper‑row reduction.**  \n   Fix \\(n\\ge 4\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   *Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n\n   *The points that remain uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal U_{n,k}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid n-k+1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal U_{n,k}\\) is a right‑angled triangular array of size \\(k\\); its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal U_{n,k}|=\\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Covering the upper triangle with sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\Bigl(n-k+1-\\frac{i}{i+1}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   *The lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \n\n   *For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal U_{n,k}\\) we have \\(b=n-k+r\\) for some \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and, by the definition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), \\(a=n+1-b=n+1-(n-k+r)=k+1-r\\). Substituting \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) into (7) with \\(i=r\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b=\\frac{r}{r+1}\\,a+\\Bigl(n-k+1-\\frac{r}{r+1}\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_r\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal U_{n,k}\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n,k}\n   =\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion for the proved range.**  \n   The construction (8) works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (the case \\(k=n-1\\) would require the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\), which would be missing; the case \\(k=n\\) would leave no horizontal line at all, and the above formulas no longer guarantee that the points of the lowest row are covered). Therefore we have established the existence of configurations for all \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\).\n\n   Together with the observations of Sections 2 and 3 we obtain the following partial classification:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{array}{ll}\n   n=3:&\\text{the only possible value is }k=0;\\\\[2mm]\n   n\\ge 4:&\\text{every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\text{ is attainable.}\n   \\end{array}}\n   \\]\n\n   The status of the two largest values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\) remains open within the present argument.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
