al P_n\\) can be partitioned into \\(n\\) sunny pairs; the case \\(k=n-1\\) remains the delicate one.)\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_3=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Slopes between points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)\n\nFor every unordered pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_3\\) compute the slope\n\\(m(P,Q)=\\dfrac{y_Q-y_P}{x_Q-x_P}\\) (vertical pairs have slope \\(\\infty\\)).\nThe fifteen slopes are listed below; the three *sunny* ones are highlighted.\n\n| Pair \\((P,Q)\\) | Slope |\n|----------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) |\n| \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(\\mathbf{-\\tfrac12}\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) | \\(\\mathbf{-2}\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(2,2)\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(\\infty\\) |\n| \\((2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\(0\\) |\n| \\((2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(-1\\) |\n\nThus the only possible sunny slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm=1,\\qquad m=-\\tfrac12,\\qquad m=-2,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nand each occurs for exactly one unordered pair:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=1 &: \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\nm=-\\tfrac12 &: \\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nm=-2 &: \\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently **any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)**, and the three pairs in (3) are the only sunny pairs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:y=1,\\qquad L_2:y=2,\\qquad L_3:y=3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll three are non‑sunny, distinct, and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_3\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x \\quad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, sunny)},\\\\\nL_2 &: x=1 \\quad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\\nL_3 &: y=1 \\quad\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,2)\\); \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(L_3\\) contains \\((2,1),(3,1)\\). Thus all six points are covered and exactly one line (\\(L_1\\)) is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nTake the three sunny lines corresponding to the three sunny pairs of (3):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (all sunny).  \n\\(L_1\\) passes through \\((1,1),(2,2)\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) passes through \\((1,2),(3,1)\\);  \n\\(L_3\\) passes through \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) lies on one of the three lines, and all three lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of three distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nBecause of (2) each sunny line can contain at most two points, the two sunny lines together cover at most four points. Consequently the remaining (non‑sunny) line must contain the two points that are not covered by the sunny lines.\n\nRemoving any two of the three sunny pairs listed in (3) leaves the complementary pair shown in the table below:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Sunny pairs removed} & \\text{Remaining pair (slope)}\\\\ \\hline\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\} & (1,3),(2,1)\\;\\;m=-2\\\\\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\} & (1,2),(3,1)\\;\\;m=-\\tfrac12\\\\\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\} & (1,1),(2,2)\\;\\;m=1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn each case the leftover pair has slope \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\) or \\(-2\\), none of which belongs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) of non‑sunny slopes. Therefore the two uncovered points cannot lie on a non‑sunny line, contradicting the assumption that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\nHence **no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe exhaustive analysis yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nas the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Pattern suggested\n\nFor the smallest admissible \\(n\\) we see that the only forbidden value is \\(k=n-1\\); the extreme value \\(k=n\\) is attainable (by partitioning \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) into \\(n\\) sunny pairs). This observation is consistent with the constructions for general \\(n\\) that realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (see the partial result proved earlier). The small‑case analysis therefore supports the conjectural full description:\n\n\\[\n\\text{For all }n\\ge3,\\qquad\nk\\text{ is possible }\\Longleftrightarrow k\\neq n-1.\n\\]\n\nThe present answer settles the case \\(n=3\\) completely and highlights the emerging pattern."
