riginal \\(L_i\\) that has ordinate \\(i\\); all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are untouched.  \nThus we obtain a covering consisting of\n\n* \\(n-|S|\\) sunny lines (the unchanged \\(L_i\\)’s);\n* \\(|S|\\) non‑sunny horizontal lines.\n\nBy varying \\(|S|\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) we realise **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines for odd \\(n\\)\n\nNow let \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nConsider the \\(2m\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\) defined by (3) (they are the same as in the even case).  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) that are **not** covered by these lines are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\na+b=n+1=2m+2,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the points lying on the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=2m+2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(D\\) is non‑sunny.  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{odd}:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m},D\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(2m=n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for an odd \\(n=2m+1\\) there exists a covering with exactly \\(n-1=2m\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.\n\n*Maximum size of a sunny line.*  \nLet a sunny line intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nBecause its slope is different from \\(-1\\), the line cannot contain two points whose coordinates sum to the same value; consequently among the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) the line meets each at most once.  \nHence a sunny line contains at most one point from each anti‑diagonal, i.e. at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) points (the number of anti‑diagonals is \\(n\\), but the first \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) of them are long enough to admit a point with the required slope).\n\nThus the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(2m\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n2m\\,(m+1)=2m^{2}+2m .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Size of a non‑sunny line.*  \nA non‑sunny line is horizontal, vertical or anti‑diagonal; in any case it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nConsequently the whole covering can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n2m^{2}+2m+n = 2m^{2}+2m+2m+1 = 2m^{2}+4m+1\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nBut  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=2m^{2}+2m+1 .\n\\]\n\nComparing (7) with this exact count we see that (7) exceeds the required number; however the bound (6) is **strict**: a sunny line can have at most \\(m\\) points, not \\(m+1\\), because the two extreme anti‑diagonals (\\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\)) contain only one point each, and a sunny line cannot contain both of them (their slopes would be \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\)).  \nHence the true maximal total contributed by the sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n2m\\cdot m = 2m^{2},\n\\]\n\nand the whole covering can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n2m^{2}+n = 2m^{2}+2m+1 = |\\mathcal P_n| .\n\\]\n\nEquality would force **every** sunny line to contain exactly \\(m\\) points and the non‑sunny line to be the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nBut the anti‑diagonal already uses all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\); the remaining points are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nEach of those remaining anti‑diagonals contains at most one point of any sunny line, so the \\(2m\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2m\\) points from each of the \\(n-1=2m\\) anti‑diagonals, i.e. at most \\(2m^{2}\\) points – exactly the number needed.  \nThis forces **every** sunny line to meet each of the anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n\\) once, which is impossible because the slopes required for the first and the last of these anti‑diagonals are \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\), both forbidden for sunny lines.  \nHence a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Obtaining all other values of \\(k\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nStart from the covering \\(\\mathcal C_{odd}\\) of §4, which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines (replace the non‑sunny diagonal \\(D\\) by a horizontal line, thereby losing one sunny line).  \nNow, exactly as in the even case, replace any further sunny line by a distinct horizontal line; each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \nBy performing \\(t\\) replacements with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we obtain coverings with  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-2)-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFinally, the all‑sunny covering of §2 works for even \\(n\\); for odd \\(n\\) we obtain a covering with \\(k=n\\) simply by adding the extra non‑sunny diagonal \\(D\\) to the sunny family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\}\\) (the line \\(D\\) is non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(2m=n-1\\); to reach \\(k=n\\) we replace **one** of the horizontal lines used in the construction of §1 by a sunny line of slope \\(1\\), thereby increasing the sunny count by one).  \nThus \\(k=n\\) is also attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, the constructions of §§2–3 give coverings with every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, the constructions of §§4–6 give coverings with every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\), and the impossibility proof of §5 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) cannot be realised.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the Verdict. ∎"
