) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{2m+1-i}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   One checks directly that for each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{2m}\\) there is a unique index \\(i\\) with \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\); indeed solving the linear equation for \\(i\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   i = a+b-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,2m\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(2m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{2m}\\) partition \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\).  \n   Consequently **all** values \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are attainable for even \\(n\\) (take the construction of \u00a74 for \\(k=n\\) and replace any subset of the lines by the non\u2011sunny lines of (2) to obtain any smaller \\(k\\)).\n\n5. **A sunny covering for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,2m\\) keep the lines \\(L_i\\) defined in (4) (they are sunny).  \n   The remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) are exactly those with \\(a+b=n+1=2m+2\\); they lie on the anti\u2011diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2m+2,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m},D\\}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(2m=n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\).\n\n   Consequently for odd \\(n\\) we have a construction with \\(k=n-1\\) **impossible** (the family (7) shows that the *only* way to obtain a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would force the non\u2011sunny line to be the anti\u2011diagonal; but that line already contains all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\), leaving the remaining points to be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which is impossible because each of those sunny lines can contain at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor=m+1\\) points, and  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   = (2m)\\,(m+1)=2m^2+2m < \\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the total capacity of the sunny lines is strictly small