e trivial covering (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \nHence a covering with **no** sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of sunny lines for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le 2m\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(2m+1-i)\\,y=(i-1)\\,x+(2m+1-i).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{2m+1-i}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Containment.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{2m}\\).  \nSet \\(s=a+b-1\\) (note that \\(1\\le s\\le 2m\\) because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\)).  \nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_s\\):\n\n\\[\n(2m+1-s)\\,b=(s-1)\\,a+(2m+1-s)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nb=\\frac{s-1}{2m+1-s}\\,a+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the defining relation of \\(L_s\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\in L_{a+b-1}\\).\n\n*Uniqueness.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) belonged to two different lines \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), then we would have \\(a+b-1=i=j\\), a contradiction.  \nHence each point of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\) lies on **exactly one** of the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{2m}\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(2m=n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) form a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with **\\(k=n\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From the all‑sunny covering to all intermediate values (even \\(n\\))\n\nTake the covering \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) of §2.  \nFor any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) replace each \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\in S\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=i\\).  \nThe new line still contains every point of the origina