(2m+1-s).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_s\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{s-1}{\\,2m+1-s\\,}\\); both numerator and denominator are positive, so the slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence every \\(L_s\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Coverage.*  Take any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{2m}\\).  \n   Set \\(s:=a+b\\) (note that \\(2\\le s\\le 2m\\)).  Substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (2m+1-s)b=(s-1)a+(2m+1-s),\n   \\]\n\n   which is an identity because \\(a+b=s\\).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(2m=n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{2m}\\) partition \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\); each point belongs to exactly one of them.\n\n5. **From the all‑sunny covering to all intermediate values (even \\(n\\)).**  \n   Start from the covering \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{2m}\\) (which has \\(k=n\\) sunny lines).  \n   For any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,2m\\}\\) replace each line \\(L_s\\) with \\(s\\in S\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=s-1\\).  \n   The horizontal line \\(y=s-1\\) contains precisely the points of \\(L_s\\) whose ordinate equals \\(s-1\\); all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\) are untouched because they lie on the unchanged lines.  \n   Hence after the replacement we still have a covering, now consisting of  \n\n   * \\(n-|S|\\) sunny lines (the unchanged \\(L_s\\)’s);\n   * \\(|S|\\) non‑sunny horizontal lines.\n\n   By varying \\(|S|\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) we obtain coverings with every integer \\(k\\) in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n6. **A covering for odd \\(n\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Use the sunny lines \\(L_s\\) defined in (2) for \\(s=2,\\dots ,2m\\) (there are \\(2m=n-1\\) of them).  \n   The points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) that are **not** covered by these lines are exactly those with  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b=n+1=2m+2,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the points lying on the anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2m+2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   \\(D\\) is non‑sunny.  Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{\\text{odd}}:=\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(2m=n-1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\).\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n\n   Suppose a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed for odd \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it meets each anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in **at most one** point.  \n   Hence a sunny line contains at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals, i.e. at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\n   If we have only \\(n-1=2m\\) sunny lines, the total number of points they can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2m\\,(m+1)=2m^{2}+2m .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_{2m+1}|=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=2m^{2}+2m+1\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points, i.e. one more than the maximum possible contribution of the sunny lines.  \n   Therefore at least one point would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence a covering with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist for odd \\(n\\).\n\n8. **Obtaining all other values of \\(k\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *From \\(k=n\\) to \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Start from the covering \\(\\mathcal C_{\\text{odd}}\\) of §6, which has \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Replace the non‑sunny diagonal \\(D\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\); this reduces the number of sunny lines by **one**, giving a covering with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *From \\(k=n-2\\) down to \\(k=0\\).*  \n   As in the even case, we may now replace any sunny line \\(L_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le 2m\\)) by the horizontal line \\(y=s-1\\).  \n   Each replacement lowers the number of sunny lines by exactly one and does not destroy the covering property, because the horizontal line still contains the points of \\(L_s\\) whose ordinate equals \\(s-1\\) and all other points remain covered by the unchanged lines.  \n   By performing \\(t\\) replacements with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we obtain coverings with  \n\n   \\[\n   k=(n-2)-t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *The case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   For odd \\(n\\) we can simply add to the covering of §6 the extra sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y = x + (m+1),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   which is distinct from all lines already used.  The line \\(S\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b-a=m+1\\); these points are already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\) introduced in the previous step, so the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one, giving a covering with \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Consequently for odd \\(n\\) all values  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   are attainable, and the only unattainable value is \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the even and odd cases yields exactly the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines stated at the beginning of the summary. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
