\\) is irrational, \\(L_{a,b}\\) contains no other lattice point, hence the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\}\\) are all distinct, all sunny, and clearly cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **A useful family of sunny lines (the “diagonal” family).**  \n   Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and write \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   For every integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; (s-1)x+(s-1)y=(s-1)^2+(s-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (3) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -x + s .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(D_s\\) is \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(D_s\\) is **non‑sunny**.  \n   The line \\(D_s\\) contains exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=s\\); i.e. the whole anti‑diagonal of sum \\(s\\).\n\n   For the **sunny** counterpart we replace the slope \\(-1\\) by a rational slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For the same integer \\(s\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_s:\\; (s-1)x-(s-1)y = (s-1)^2-(s-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (5) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x - (s-2).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_s\\) is \\(+1\\); consequently every \\(S_s\\) is **sunny**.  \n   Moreover, a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(S_s\\) **iff** \\(a-b = s-2\\).\n\n5. **Even \\(n\\).  Construction for every \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m\\).  \n   *Sunny part.*  Choose any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le m\\).  \n   Take the \\(t\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_t=\\{S_{2},S_{3},\\dots ,S_{t+1}\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S_t\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  Use the \\(n-t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_{n-t}= \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-t\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-t\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H_{n-t}\\).\n\n   Because for every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{2m}\\) we have either  \n   \\[\n   b\\le n-t\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\},\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   the family \\(\\mathcal S_t\\cup\\mathcal H_{n-t}\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\).  \n   The family consists of exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=t\\).  \n   Since \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), **every** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is attainable when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n6. **Odd \\(n\\).  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (so \\(m\\ge1\\)).  \n   Suppose a covering with exactly \\(n-1=2m\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it meets each anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   Hence a sunny line contains at most one point from each anti‑diagonal, i.e. at most one point for each value of \\(s\\).  \n   There are \\(n\\) different anti‑diagonals, so the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(2m\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2m\\cdot n = 2m(2m+1)=4m^{2}+2m .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal P_{2m+1}|=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 = 2m^{2}+2m+1 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(4m^{2}+2m > 2m^{2}+2m+1\\) for every \\(m\\ge1\\), the bound (10) is **not** restrictive; the real restriction comes from the fact that a sunny line cannot contain two points belonging to the same *row* (i.e. with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate) nor two points belonging to the same *column* (same \\(x\\)-coordinate).  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = m+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m+1}\\) (the maximum is attained when the line passes through the points \\((1,m+1),(2,m),\\dots ,(m+1,1)\\)).  \n\n   Hence the \\(2m\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2m\\,(m+1)=2m^{2}+2m\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   points, which is **one short** of the required \\(2m^{2}+2m+1\\) points.  \n   Therefore a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n7. **Odd \\(n\\).  Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\).  \n\n   *Base covering ( \\(k=n\\) ).*  \n   As in step 3 we may take the \\(n\\) irrational‑slope lines (2); this gives \\(k=n\\).\n\n   *Covering with \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Start from the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C_{0}= \\{\\,D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{2m+1}\\,\\},\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the \\(n\\) non‑sunny anti‑diagonals.  \n   Replace the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{2m+2}\\) (which does **not** belong to \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) because its sum exceeds \\(n+1\\)) by the sunny line \\(S_{2}\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)).  \n   The resulting family contains \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines and one sunny line, i.e. \\(k=1\\).  \n   By repeatedly replacing additional anti‑diagonals \\(D_{s}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le 2m+1\\)) with the corresponding sunny line \\(S_{s}\\) we increase the number of sunny lines by one each time while never losing the covering property (the points of the replaced anti‑diagonal are still covered because they lie on the new sunny line).  \n   After performing \\(t\\) such replacements we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines, for every \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Obtaining \\(k=n\\).*  \n   The construction of step 3 already gives \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Consequently, for odd \\(n\\) all values  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   are attainable, and the only unattainable value is \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the even and odd cases we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines stated at the beginning of the summary. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. 