            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*Lemma 1 (Non‑sunny covering).*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line\n\\[\nL_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\nThe slope of \\(L_{s}\\) is \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_{s}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence each \\(L_{s}\\) is non‑sunny.  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Set \\(s=a+b\\). Then \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{s}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_{s}\\). Because the representation \\(s=a+b\\) is unique, each such point belongs to exactly one line among \\(\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\).  \n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines that together contain every point of the set\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Lemma 2 (Sunny covering).*  \nFix \\(n\\ge3\\). For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) choose an irrational number \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) such that the \\(\\alpha_{i}\\)’s are pairwise distinct (for instance \\(\\alpha_{i}=\\sqrt{2}+i\\)). Define the line\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_{i}\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) satisfies (4), so it lies on \\(S_{i}\\).  \n\nSuppose that a second lattice point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) also lies on \\(S_{i}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{y_{0}-(n+1-i)}{x_{0}-i}=\\alpha_{i}.\n\\]\nThe left‑hand side is a ratio of two integers, hence rational, whereas \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) is irrational. This contradiction shows that no such second lattice point exists. Consequently each line \\(S_{i}\\) contains **exactly one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), namely its anchor \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\n\nThe slope \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor undefined nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny. The lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.  \n\nThe set of anchors \\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) coincides with the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\): indeed any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); if equality holds then \\((a,b)=(i,n+1-i)\\) for \\(i=a\\). If the inequality is strict, then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\); however such a point cannot belong to any of the lines \\(S_{i}\\) because each \\(S_{i}\\) contains only its anchor. Hence the only points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) are the anchors themselves. (A direct verification shows that the cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\); the anchors provide exactly \\(n\\) points, and the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points are covered implicitly because each of them lies on one of the non‑sunny lines \\(L_{s}\\) from Lemma 1, which we may add without changing the number of sunny lines. However, for the purpose of exhibiting a configuration with **exactly** \\(n\\) sunny lines we keep only the sunny family \\(\\{S_{i}\\}\\); the definition of “cover’’ in the problem statement requires that **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lie on at least one of the chosen lines. Since each \\(S_{i}\\) contains only its anchor, the family \\(\\{S_{i}\\}\\) alone does **not** cover the